1. e4 {At the outset of the final round, Thomas only needed a draw to be sure of the title, while Wise had to win. The game was worthy of the occasion as Wise made a determined effort to win.} e5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. d4 Nxe4 4. Bd3 d5 5. Nxe5 Bd6 6. O-O O-O 7. Nc3 Nxc3 8. bxc3 Nd7 9. f4 f5 10. c4 c5 {Fighting for an equal share of the centre, and definitely the right strategy.} 11. c3 Nb6 12. Qb3 Be6 13. cxd5 Bxd5 14. c4 Be4 15. d5 Qc7 16. Bb2 Nd7 17. Nxd7 Qxd7 18. Be5 Rf7 19. Bxe4 fxe4 20. Rae1 Re8 21. Rxe4 Bxe5 22. fxe5 Rxf1+ 23. Kxf1 Rf8+ 24. Ke2 {The critical position.} ({If} 24. Kg1 Qf5 25. Qf3 {I consider White has good winning prospects. If Black exchanges Queens the united passed pawns look very strong. As played, the White King becomes too exposed to avoid checks.}) 24... Qf5 25. Qf3 Qg5 26. Qg4 Qc1 {Sources: bare game score, David LeMoir, 2018; score with JM Aitken's notes: WECU website} 1/2-1/2

